   333	## 5. Self-pair cubic and the Betti obstruction
   334	
   335	Let `t!=u`, set
   336	
   337	```text
   338	s=t+u,       p=t*u.
   339	```
   340	
   341	Dividing `U(t)-U(u)` and `V(t)-V(u)` by `t-u` and rewriting symmetrically
   342	gives two equations.  The first is
   343	
   344	```text
   345	c+2h*s-2p*s+s^3=0.                                     (5.1)
   346	```
   347	
   348	Eliminating `p` from the pair gives
   349	
   350	```text
   351	s^4(s^3+4h*s-c)=0.                                     (5.2)
   352	```
   353	
   354	The value `s=0` is impossible in (5.1) because `c!=0`.  Conversely, every
   355	root of
   356	
   357	```text
   358	H(s)=s^3+4h*s-c                                         (5.3)
   359	```
   360	
   361	solves both divided difference equations after setting
   362	
   363	```text
   364	p=c/s-h.                                                (5.4)
   365	```
   366	
   367	The endpoint quadratic `z^2-sz+p` has discriminant
   368	
   369	```text
   370	s^2-4p=-3c/s,                                           (5.5)
   371	```
   372	
   373	where (5.3) was used.  It is nonzero, so every root of `H` supplies a
   374	genuine unordered pair of distinct normalization points.  Distinct roots
   375	give distinct pairs because their sums differ.
   376	
   377	A cubic has only one distinct root exactly when it has a triple root.  But
   378	a triple root of (5.3) would satisfy `H''(s)=6s=0`, hence `s=0`, contradicting
   379	`H(0)=-c!=0`.  Therefore at least two distinct unordered self-pairs exist.
