   389	Take the two distinct pairs above.  If their image values differ, (5.6)
   390	gets at least `1+1`.  If their image values agree, their union contains at
   391	least three distinct normalization points (two distinct two-element subsets
   392	cannot have a two-element union), and that one fibre contributes at least
   393	`3-1`.  In both cases
   394	
   395	```text
   396	b1(B)>=2.                                               (5.7)
   397	```
   398	
   399	Repeated roots of `H` do not create an escape.  They record tangency or
   400	collision multiplicity, while (5.5) keeps the two endpoints distinct; and
   401	the no-triple-root argument still leaves at least two distinct pairs.
