   217	## 4. Why cubic ramification escapes the conductor
   218	
   219	Let `{alpha,beta}` be the unique two-point normalization fibre.  Since the
   220	two points have the same image, they have the same `X` value.  The derivative
   221	of the cubic `X` has degree two.
   222	
   223	If its two roots were the two distinct conductor endpoints, each endpoint
   224	would have local degree at least two in the same cubic fibre, requiring
   225	length at least `2+2>3`.  If `X'` has one double root at a conductor endpoint,
   226	that endpoint has local degree three; its distinct conductor mate adds one,
   227	again requiring `3+1>3`.  Thus some critical parameter
   228	
   229	```text
   230	gamma notin {alpha,beta}                               (4.1)
   231	```
   232	
   233	always exists.  Equation (3.5) also shows that `nu(gamma)` cannot share its
   234	image with another normalization point.  It may nevertheless be a
   235	point-bijective unibranch singularity.  This is why the proof must use the
   236	charged local fibre table rather than an unsupported immersion claim.
   237	
   238	For the charged `m=0,n4=0` packet, `nu(gamma)` is not the unique `(2,2)`
   239	value and is therefore `(2,1,1)`, with complete local group `C2`.  A local
   240	projection frame contains two meridians from the ramified cluster when the
   241	local degree is two, or all three when it is three.  All nontrivial meridians
   242	in that `C2` are the same transposition.  The local three-tuple consequently
   243	has a duplicate.
   244	
   245	Three transpositions generate a transitive subgroup of `S4` exactly when
   246	their support edges form a spanning tree on four letters.  A duplicate leaves
   247	at most two distinct edges and is intransitive.  Finally, an elementary
   248	Hurwitz move
   249	
   250	```text
   251	(A,B) |-> (A*B*A^(-1),A)                               (4.2)
   252	```
   253	
   254	is a Nielsen transformation.  It and its inverse preserve the subgroup
   255	generated by the whole tuple.  Therefore an arbitrary full braid tail between
   256	the original based frame and the local frame cannot restore `S4`.  This is the
   257	valid repair of the older false shortcut that tried to identify individual
   258	original colors across different tails.
   259	
   260	Nothing in this argument applies at a `(3,1)` point with local group `S3`.
   261	There, three local meridians may map to overlapping transpositions and already
   262	generate `S3`; the `m=1` horn survives this packet.
   263	
