   120	This is a pointwise stabilizer computation, not merely a divisorial inertia
   121	calculation.  It therefore checks conductor points and singular codimension-
   122	two points as well.  No hidden ramification remains in the `|T31|=0,n4=0`
   123	row, while the unique `(3,1)` point in the other row has the whole `A3`
   124	stabilizer.
   125	
   126	## 4. Etale class and local-extension firewall
   127	
   128	When `|T31|=0`, (3.2) makes the `A3` action free at every affine point.
   129	Thus `W->D` is a finite-etale degree-three torsor.  It is connected because
   130	`M` is a field, so it is nontrivial.  An odd element of `S3` induces the
   131	double-plane involution and conjugates a three-cycle to its inverse, proving
   132	(0.1).
   133	
   134	After identifying `Z/3` with `mu_3`, Kummer gives
   135	
   136	```text
   137	0 -> O(D)^*/O(D)^{*3}
   138	  -> H^1_et(D,Z/3)
   139	  -> Pic(D)[3]
   140	  -> 0.                                                 (4.1)
   141	```
   142	
   143	Thus the class can come from a unit or a three-torsion line bundle.  `Cl[3]`
   144	is not the Kummer target; a class-group computation suffices only through the
   145	injection `Pic(D)->Cl(D)` or a separate local-Cartier analysis.
   146	
   147	A class on `D_reg`, a curve complement, or a punctured singular link does
   148	not automatically extend over `D`.  Its restriction to every punctured
   149	strict-henselian singular neighborhood must be trivial.  The forced class in
   150	the no-cusp row passes this gate because it already comes from the globally
   151	finite-etale map.  The ordinary-cusp model `UV=X^3` explains the other row:
   152	its `C3` cover is free off the vertex and fixed at the vertex, so it is only
   153	quasi-etale.
